<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[Placeholder]</span></p>
<p>Hey, author, let’s take a look.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Feedback</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Self aware NPC attempts to break out to the real world</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Refrain from calling the SCP an “NPC”. It’s not a programmed character, it’s a sentient or semi-sentient non-physical entity.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>after Foundation tries to contain it, and learns about multi-layered nature of the Foundation reality, created specifically to stop pataphysical threats</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you’re talking about multiple realities, then we have to clarify some things. First, the SCP multiverse wasn’t created by the Foundation to stop certain types of anomalies. The multiverse has many different stories about its origin, but the Foundation never had the power to create a whole multiverse. And second, I don’t actually think that a self aware RPG character would be able to exist in multiple realities nor have the power to travel between them. Making your SCP overpowered is kinda cliche and a really easy way of getting downvoted.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>whose name is a cognitohazard, that forces people perceiving it to include this character in their roleplaying activities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would his name be a cognitohazard? And if he is a rpg character, how would the Foundation know that he is sentient? If you’re taking about paper and pencil rpg, then it’s basically impossible for a character to be sentient, since the person using that character would choose everything they would do. Also, just because a certain character is played multiple times by multiple people doesn’t mean it’s an anomaly, it could mean that a certain character is really famous and people like using him in their roleplaying activities.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>but we don't have any coming from &quot;below&quot; and on purpose.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Like I said before, the Foundation isn’t able to build multiple realities, and if they could send entities through these layers, then it would be a infinite loop of the same entity traveling each time to other layers, including our own. The reason why is that, since there are infinite realities, then there is one “below” us with that same entity, and it would eventually reach us again. That’s probably the worst way of containing an SCP, since they would always come back.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm also planning on exploring limits of the mentioned cognitohazard, and the entity itself through test logs</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you’re going to explore that, you need to describe everything here. That means you need to describe all the effects it causes.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;The&nbsp;actual&nbsp;idea</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It’s not that interesting to me. I feel this is some kind of bad mix of <a href="/scp-3812">SCP-3812</a>, <a href="/scp-079">SCP-079</a> and Monika from DDLC. The idea of using other realities is also flawed, since most of it doesn’t make sense. Also, I don’t think an entity that lives inside a roleplaying game could do much, since I believe he would be bound by the laws of that game. So basically, no matter if it’s a paper and pencil rpg or a computer game, it would never be able to leave its realm. I recommend you to remodel the concept and try different things, because I don’t think it would be greenlighted as it is. Good luck! ;)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

