					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mic4u" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7191085); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7191085&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610117187" alt="Mic4u" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7191085)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mic4u" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7191085); return false;" >Mic4u</a></span> <span class="odate time_1610052251 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Jan 2021 20:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4864579">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Still, why would the Foundation keep making that entity travel between other stories/realities? The reason why I'm asking this is because, as far as I've seen, this entity doesn't have the power to go into the real world, regardless of the story he's in. So it basically poses no threat at all, as long as people doesn't know his name and nobody plays as him. [&#8230;] Destroy everything regarding him and leave at least one thing about him inside one of the Foundation sites. Testing would always end up with amnestics, so it should be okay;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's exactly the Fundations plan on containing it. They just need to track down all places where its name is present, and since new ones can't apear randomly, it is still relatievly easy to contain, at least in theory. The thing is that it was pretty much all over the place (nobody really noticed, beacuse of the stuff you mentioned earlier, it seems like just a popular character, that people get really immersed in while playing it, except for those extreme cases I mentioned above) so taking it all down will take some time. Internet is not the problem, it can be constantly monitored, but a lot of information between RPG players is exchanged by simply talking on meet-ups, convencions etc. so it's kind of like tracking down a plague, trying to find all &quot;infected&quot; before they spread the memetic &quot;disease&quot;.</p>
<p>So, so far most of SCPs activity happens during those unsupervised sessions, outside of Fundations control (technicalyy fundation could just amnesticise all nerds on Earth, but I doubt that such relatievly minor SCP threat would warrant such a high scale operation). During testing it's posibilites are always limited, for example by putting it, narratievly, in a empty cell room with nothing the SCP could possibly use<br />
In a nutshell - that's exactly what Fundation tries to do, and they are mostly succeding, they just need time to cut off all branches through which the cognitohazard has spread.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So the entity is able to have a little bit of control over the player?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, altough the control doesn't extend to the &quot;real world&quot;, for example if you finish the session with your friends, the SCP goes away, untill the next time you &quot;let it in&quot; by starting to play. And you really want to do that because of the compulsion. Most dangerous are LARPs, where the SCP technicaly controls the whole body of the player, and has the most &quot;power&quot; in the real world.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The transcendence to our reality stuff</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think I see your point and I agree, the breaking out to higher reality seems to cause more problems than it's worth in this idea. When I think about it he does seem a bit OP, and reminds of some badly written Appolyons, where we can't do anything, it will jsut come out and devour us all!</p>
<p>He can't really get to our world without outside help, and any way for him to achieve it would feel a bit handwavy. I think the best way to go now, its to go back to the original idea and treat it as a starting point to something more interesting. I think I have an idea where I can take this concept, I'll present it at the end of this post, so you can judge it better.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">So&nbsp;who&nbsp;is&nbsp;this&nbsp;guy&nbsp;and&nbsp;what&nbsp;he&nbsp;can&nbsp;do&nbsp;anyway</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Shortly, he is a hoarder of magical and technological artifacts, propably to a pathological degree. He is kind of like those players, that collects every frickin basket or other piece of junk, only with the diference that it has to be somehow extraordynary. He mostly appears in two roles: as a quest giver, that tasks players with getting him a specific item, or as a person that tries to get it themselves, either with the help of players (in this case he propably is someones character they play as), or without it.</p>
<p>Why does he collect all this stuff? I thought about giving him some symphatetic backstory, similar to &quot;oh, he needs those artifacts to buy his loved one from someone, or free them from somewhere&quot; but I think it's an overused trope, so I like the idea of him collecting those things because he likes to simply collect stuff. It just scratches the right spots in his brain, similarly to when a shopaholic buys something new.</p>
<p>He is clever, but not to the level of Sherlock Holmes, or some &quot;so clever its dangerous&quot; SCPs like <a href="http://scp-wiki.net.pl/scp-035">SCP-035</a>. He likes to talk and boast about his collection, which provides him a lot of interactions while somebody roleplays as him, but I don't think I would call him charismatic. One of the Titles he uses is &quot;Child of the Fey&quot; which propably is the reason why his name is cognitohazardous.</p>
<p>Other ascpect of his name I didn't had the oportunity to mention, but is kind of easy to predict: all you need to see is a name of this SCP (also, nicknames, titles and descriptions don't work, so he can be called SCP-XXXX safely) and somehow you will &quot;know&quot; all the information about him, the artifacts he posseses, his memories and such. For the host it will feel like he is just improvising, coming up with those ideas on spot, but they will fit together perfectly with the ones created by other people, even if they didn't had a way to exchange information.</p>
<p>Most of his powers, except mentioned cognitohazardous nature of his name, and partial mind control he has over his &quot;hosts&quot;, come mostly from the artifacts he posseses, but they have impact practically only on the fictional worlds, not the real one. Since most of them are magical trinkets of rather low power, in the article I plan on only making a list of kind of honorable mentions. That's the artifacts I thought about so far:<br />
- Bag of holding, where he keeps his stuff<br />
- Ring of Gygax, that grants invisibility (for thieving obviously), which is an intentional misspeling of Ring of Gyges from Greek mythology<br />
- set of clothing that transforms to fit in with where he is right now (space suit in space, fancy clothes on some fantasy quasi-medieval court)<br />
- Box of colorfull chalk, that allows him to kind of travel to difrent fictional universes. He has to take a piece of chalk, draw a circle, grind and eat the reamining part of the piece, and go to sleep in this circle. During that an identical copy of him will be created in random fictional world (the one he is about to be roleplayed as in) while leaving the original where he was. All copies share memories, and this way can be present in few places at the same time (multiple roleplaying activites happening simultaniously) and they will know what happens at difrent ones. It also prevents SCP from dying permamently in fictional universe (you can say &quot;a rock falls on you and you die&quot; and it will kill this instance of the SCP, but many others exist, only way to get rid of him for good is to simply not play as him, and erease all info about him.)</p>
<p>Another quality of him is his resistance to change originating from upper world. You can't really tell &quot;you decided to stop spreading to other worlds and tell us about all places where you are right now&quot;, I mean, you can, but it won't work, SCP just won't acknoledge something that directly influences his free will.</p>
<p>I think that's all a have about him so far.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">New&nbsp;idea&nbsp;for&nbsp;the&nbsp;overarching&nbsp;plot</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>While I was writing description of the SCP above I noticed something funky.<br />
He is, in a way, very similar to the SCP Fundation.<br />
He collects &quot;magical stuff&quot; for no particular reason. Fundation contains anomalies also for no clear reason. (some people say &quot;to protect humanity&quot;, if that was the case I think the benefical SCP would be more widespread in the everyday lives of normal people).</p>
<p>So the new idea is that the SCP spreads as it did in the original idea. One of the more hardcore examples of its influence (idk, beating someone to death during a LARP over some &quot;magical artifact&quot; that was just a prop in real world) Foundation learns about it, they investigate, notice its anomalous and try to contain it. They delete all they can find on the interent, they look for potentialy exposed people and give them amnestics (amnestics make you forget the name, and without the name the effects disapear, I don't think I mentioned that), and at the same time they test this thing through making some D-class roleplay as the SCP.<br />
The SCP is curious what the Foundation is, especially that he notices that there is a huge drop in the number of &quot;active universes&quot; he is in. He agrees to exchange of information, he will tell a bit about himslef, and Foundation will tell a bit about themselves. He doesn't learn that much, but he learns three important things:</p>
<ol>
<li>The Fundation wants to contain him/stop him from aquiring more objects (actually from interfering with others people lives and beeing a anomaly roaming free, but he mostly cares about the first part)</li>
<li>The Foundation contains anomalous entities (similar to him) and anomalous objects (similarly to him)</li>
<li>Foundation exists in a world above his, so he can't really influence it directly, while Foundation, and other people from this world can influence his.</li>
</ol>
<p>When he learns about other SCPs he really wants to get them for himslef, but knows its imposible, so he want a second best things: ideal fictional copies of the SCPs. Even though Foundation technicalyy wouldn't give away anything real, this SCP can freely send information to his other iterations, and that would cause a major security breach, when all the clasiffied info would be leaked by the SCP, so the Foundation obviously doesn't allow it (they made the information exchange before they knew about his ability to send information to diferent iterations of himself. They can't amnesticise him, that would only work on the one copy. This makes Foundation only want to contain it more, luckily there wasn't much that was leaked, and it wasn't that credible).</p>
<p>Then SCP comes up with the idea. He was kinda aware of the &quot;upper world&quot; he just didnt really cared about it, since, from what he knew, there was no magical gizmos he could get. He decides to try to influence people he has contact with, by breaking the fourth wall, and telling them to get him info on the Foundation and then put the SCPs into the games they are running, because he just want's to get the copies of the SCPs for himself</p>
<p>So the geeks and nerds under influence of the SCP try to get information about Foundation. They may try to contact GoIs, or work on their own (propably some GoIs have some RPG fans in their ranks too).</p>
<p>Why would they do that, what leverage does the SCP have? Mayby he lied to them and said that there is a way to drag the original into fictional world, not just make the copy of it. So he could convince all those poeple that they could Dungeon the Dragons for real, it would be their &quot;real life&quot;, not the climatic crisis and student loans. He could give them the world of adventure. It would propably work even better, since he will be able to convince them that their world is also &quot;not real&quot;, there might be one above it, so what deiference does it make if you live in a fantasy on the first level, or the second one, they are equally not real.</p>
<p>He doesn't want to move <strong>upvards</strong>. He wants to take stuff he likes <strong>downwards</strong>. Mayby I'll even leave the ascesion as a red hering, the Foundation expectations towards his plan, and then subvert it.</p>
<p>How do you like this new idea? Its obviously just a draft, and propably has a dozen and a half of plotholes, but is it better than the pervoius one, more interesting, or at least has more potential?</p>
<p>And again, I really can't express how gratefull I am for the time you spend workshoping this idea with me. Thank you a lot ^^</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

